#requirement(title: [Must Rule Out Before Diagnosing ME/CFS])[

*Hypothyroidism:*

    - *Symptoms*: Fatigue, cognitive impairment ("brain fog"), cold intolerance, weight gain, constipation
    - *Distinguishing features*: Gradual onset, no post-exertional malaise, responds to thyroid replacement
    - *Testing*: TSH, free T4; if TSH elevated and free T4 low, hypothyroidism is confirmed
    - *Note*: Subclinical hypothyroidism (mildly elevated TSH with normal T4) is controversial; may contribute to fatigue but is insufficient to explain ME/CFS severity

*Addison Disease (Primary Adrenal Insufficiency):*

    - *Symptoms*: Profound fatigue, orthostatic hypotension, salt craving, hyperpigmentation
    - *Distinguishing features*: Progressive worsening, life-threatening if untreated, responds to cortisol replacement
    - *Testing*: Morning cortisol, ACTH stimulation test; electrolytes show hyponatremia and hyperkalemia

*Diabetes Mellitus:*

    - *Symptoms*: Fatigue, polyuria, polydipsia, weight loss
    - *Testing*: Fasting glucose, HbA1c

*Hyperparathyroidism:*

    - *Symptoms*: Fatigue, cognitive impairment, depression, muscle weakness, thirst and polyuria, bone/joint pain, mild confusion — overlapping ME/CFS and fibromyalgia @Costa2016hyperpara
    - *Distinguishing features*: Fatigue with hypercalcaemia and elevated or inappropriately-normal parathyroid hormone (PTH); absent post-exertional malaise; symptoms can be partially reversible after parathyroidectomy @Adkisson2014hyperpara @Liu2021hyperpara
    - *Testing*: Serum calcium (part of the CMP); if elevated, confirm with PTH. The diagnostic algorithm Step 2's CMP already includes calcium
    - *Recognition*: Hyperparathyroidism is uncommon and often diagnosed by a specialist; a GP may not recognise it, but a raised calcium on any blood test must always be investigated further
    - *Note*: Routine screening yield is low in the general fatigue population @Ferrari2015hyperpara, but exclusion is warranted because primary hyperparathyroidism is surgically correctable and a missed case would otherwise be mislabelled ME/CFS @Wootton2026hyperpara
    - *Treatment*: Parathyroidectomy for primary hyperparathyroidism; oral vitamin D (colecalciferol) correction for the common secondary cause of low vitamin D; cinacalcet or bisphosphonates only where surgery is unsuitable
    - *Note*: NICE NG206 lists calcium and phosphate among investigations to exclude other diagnoses when ME/CFS is suspected

] <req:endocrine-exclusions>
